  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 5. Results\n\n**Identity (exact).** At L=1 the six L=2 columns collapse to three, `G = (-Q^jQ_i,\\ Q^jQ_i^{[-2]},\\ Q^{j[2]}Q_i)` against `w' = (Delta c_1, a_1, a_2)`. The `E_2` form and the `M` form agree term by term for arbitrary constant `Delta T_1` and linear `Delta T_2` on arbitrary test functions, symbolically in `theta, h`.\n\n**Sampling.** The three residues sample the direct functions at `n = -1,0,1,2` and the duals at `n = 0,1,2,3` — exactly the reach of `Psi^B` and `Psi^A` when all three separated variables run over the three poles.\n\n**The row triple is forced, not chosen.** Each row `(i,j)` contributes one `Q_i` and one `Q^j`. Matching the content of `Psi^B` (two `Q_1`, one `Q_2`) and of `Psi^A` (two `Q^1`, one `Q^2`) requires both multisets to be `{1,1,2}`; of the 84 triples exactly one is non-degenerate:\n\n$$\\{(i,j)\\} \\;=\\; \\{(1,1),\\,(1,2),\\,(2,1)\\}.$$\n\nThe four-triple span diagnostic agrees: direct side lies in `span Psi^B` iff the `i`-multiset is `{1,1,2}`, dual side lies in `span Psi^A` iff the `j`-multiset is.\n\n**Multilinear expansion.** `det N = sum_{n1,n2,n3} m_{n1}m_{n2}m_{n3} det[g(n1);g(n2);g(n3)]`, verified against `Det[rows]` for all 84 triples.\n\n**The diagonal terms are exactly wave functions.** For `n1 = n2 = n3 = n`,\n\n$$\\det\\big[g(n);g(n);g(n)\\big] \\;=\\; \\Psi^A(n,n,n)\\;\\Psi^B(n,n,n) \\qquad (n = 0,1,2),$$\n\nwith `Psi^A` the exact mirror of `Psi^B`, the shift `-h` replaced by `+h`. This is an identity, not a fit.\n\n**The off-diagonal terms are not.** The remaining 24 terms do not factor into wave functions (18 of the 27 dual factors and 18 of the 27 direct factors are not of `Psi` form; some terms do not even split into a dual times a direct polynomial). Concretely, `det N` has 78 monomials, the diagonal part 12, the remainder 70. The exact linear solve for\n\n$$\\det N \\;=\\; \\sum_{x \\in \\{0,h,2h\\}^3} m(x)\\,\\Psi^A(x)\\,\\Psi^B(x)$$\n\nhas **no solution** for any of the four candidate triples, for arbitrary weights `m(x)`.\n\n**Reading.** With `Q_i` and `Q^j` fully independent symbols the determinant is a rank-16 bilinear form on `span Psi^A` x `span Psi^B` (both 17-dimensional), whose diagonal is right but whose off-diagonal does not vanish. The natural suspect is that \"abstract\" is too weak: the duals are not independent of the directs — in gl(3) they are the `2x2` Casoratians `Q^1 ~ Q_{23}`, `Q^2 ~ Q_{13}`, `Q^3 ~ Q_{12}` — and the off-diagonal terms are expected to cancel only once that (or the Baxter equation) is imposed.\n\n## 6. Open\n\n1. Impose the gl(3) QQ / Hodge relation between `Q^j` and `Q_i` and re-run the diagonal fit.\n2. If that is still not enough, instantiate real Baxter solutions at L=1 with a twist and check whether the off-diagonal cancellation is genuinely on-shell.\n3. The SoV lattice for `lambda = {2,1,0}` has 8 Gelfand-Tsetlin points, not the 27 of the cube `{0,h,2h}^3` — the correct sum may be over the GT lattice with a non-trivial measure.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
